[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Hash Functions, Merkle Trees, and State Accumulators in Web3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cryptographic primitives form the foundational bedrock of Web3 and blockchain architectures. Understanding how hash functions, Merkle trees, and state accumulators work together is essential for building scalable, verifiable decentralized applications."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Cryptographic Hash Functions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A cryptographic hash function takes an input of arbitrary size and maps it to a fixed-size string of bytes. In blockchain systems like Ethereum, Keccak-256 is predominantly used. A secure hash function must possess three critical properties: preimage resistance, second preimage resistance, and collision resistance."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const { ethers } = require('ethers');
const message = 'Hello Web3';
const hash = ethers.keccak256(ethers.toUtf8Bytes(message));
console.log('Hash:', hash);"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Merkle Trees"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Merkle tree is a binary tree in which every leaf node is labeled with the cryptographic hash of a data block, and every non-leaf node is labeled with the cryptographic hash of its child labels. Merkle trees allow efficient and secure verification of large contents."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Efficiency: Verification requires O(log n) data instead of O(n)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integrity: Any tampering with a leaf node alters the root hash completely."},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"Implementing a Merkle Proof in JavaScript"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const { MerkleTree } = require('merkletreejs');
const SHA256 = require('crypto-js/sha256');

const leaves = ['data1', 'data2', 'data3', 'data4'].map(x => SHA256(x));
const tree = new MerkleTree(leaves, SHA256);
const root = tree.getRoot().toString('hex');

console.log('Merkle Root:', root);"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"3. State Accumulators"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"State accumulators are cryptographic data structures that represent a massive set of values with a single short cryptographic commitment. They allow users to prove membership or non-membership without downloading the entire state."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Verkle Trees and Future Ethereum State"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ethereum is actively researching the transition from Merkle Patricia Trees to Verkle Trees. Verkle trees use polynomial commitments to vastly reduce witness sizes, enabling stateless clients and lowering the hardware barriers for running full nodes."}]
